00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:30.080
Now, to really understand the summit dynamics at play here,

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.860
most coverage treats presidential remarks

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.940
at NATO gatherings as simple policy announcements.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.640
Yet the process of turning words into binding actions

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.820
involves multiple layers of consultation

00:06:41.820 --> 00:06:43.320
and domestic approval.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.440
In practice, a leader's public position

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:49.240
at the summit table initiates internal reviews

00:06:49.240 --> 00:06:52.120
by member states foreign and defense ministries,

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.120
followed by formal working group negotiations

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.360
that can stretch months before any joint communicate

00:06:58.360 --> 00:06:59.380
is finalized.

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.580
Historical precedent shows that even strongly worded proposals

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:08.100
such as past U.S. requests for increased European defense spending

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:12.140
only become operational after Parliament's appropriate funds

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.900
and military planners adjust force posture.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.500
Knowing this, when you hear claims that a single speech

00:07:18.500 --> 00:07:21.100
has immediately altered alliance policy,

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:23.480
you can check whether follow-up meetings

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:25.360
or budget documents have appeared.

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:28.940
Next time you see a headline about a summit declaration

00:07:28.940 --> 00:07:32.960
ask which national legislatures must still approve funding.

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.460
What existing treaties already constrained the proposal

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.780
and how long similar initiatives have taken to move

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:41.320
from rhetoric to reality?

